            <!-- Rich Text Body -->
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'zh'">
                <p>TikTok Shop 新品冷启动时，素材数量并不是越多越好。真正有效的素材池，应当让每个视频承担明确任务：吸引停留、解释卖点、证明效果、推动点击或促成下单。</p>
<h2>1. 新品刚上线，素材池至少准备多少条？</h2>
<p>建议先准备 <strong>15—25条基础素材</strong>，而不是一次制作上百条。冷启动阶段更需要快速找到有效内容组合，而不是追求素材数量。</p>
<p>可以按以下方式拆分：</p>
<ul>
<li>3—5条产品开场：直接展示商品、使用场景或痛点；</li>
<li>3—5条功能演示：解决“它有什么用”；</li>
<li>3—5条用户证明：突出真实体验、前后对比或评价；</li>
<li>3—5条促销场景：说明优惠、组合装或限时权益；</li>
<li>2—5条达人原生内容：保留达人表达，用于授权投放或Spark Ads测试。</li>
</ul>
<p>这些只是首轮测试规模，不应被当成固定标准。如果商品客单价高、决策周期长，应增加使用教程、对比测评和常见问题素材；如果商品冲动性强，则可多测试不同开场、场景和达人风格。</p>
<h2>2. 每条素材只讲一个卖点，还是完整介绍商品？</h2>
<p>新品冷启动建议采用 <strong>“一条素材一个核心卖点”</strong> 的结构。TikTok用户通常先被具体场景或问题吸引，再决定是否继续观看。</p>
<p>例如，一款便携榨汁杯可以分别测试：</p>
<ol>
<li>早晨时间紧张的用户如何快速带走早餐；</li>
<li>清洗是否方便；</li>
<li>容量是否适合通勤；</li>
<li>实际出汁效果；</li>
<li>与普通杯子的使用差异。</li>
</ol>
<p>不要在15秒视频里同时塞入材质、容量、折扣、品牌故事等全部信息。卖点越集中，越容易判断用户究竟对什么感兴趣。</p>
<h2>3. 素材先发自然流量，还是直接投广告验证？</h2>
<p>两者应并行，而不是等待自然流量跑出结果后再投。发布前可用自然流量观察开头留存和评论反馈，但自然播放不能替代广告数据，因为它受到账号权重、推荐人群和流量环境等多种因素影响。</p>
<p>更稳妥的做法是：同一批素材提前编号，采用相近文案、受众和出价进行小预算分组测试。优先比较点击率、视频停留、转化率和单个订单成本，而不是只看出价能否跑出订单。</p>
<p>首轮测试时，只改变一个主要变量。例如测试不同钩子时，不要同时更换视频长度、音乐、落地页和优惠设置。否则即使结果不同，也很难判断有效原因。</p>
<h2>4. 多个TikTok Shop账号，应该如何分配素材？</h2>
<p>新品期通常不建议仅为了“起量”拆分大量账户。账户过多会增加充值、余额监控、权限管理和素材归因难度，还可能造成预算浪费。</p>
<p>更合理的方式是：</p>
<ul>
<li>一个主账户承担新品探索和核心销售；</li>
<li>确有需要时，再建立少量账户区分不同国家、达人授权内容或独立预算；</li>
<li>达人素材优先通过官方授权工具管理，避免重复开户或违规搬运；</li>
<li>建立素材编号表，记录素材ID、版本、卖点、授权范围、消耗和订单来源；</li>
<li>为广告账户设置余额预警、充值负责人和每日消耗上限。</li>
</ul>
<p>素材表现好时，可以基于同一用户洞察迭代第二批视频，而不是盲目更换账户。跨账户复制时，也要检查授权、落地页和促销信息是否一致。</p>
<h2>5. 素材跑不动，是素材问题还是账户问题？</h2>
<p>先看数据发生在哪一步。</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>停留低、点击率低</strong>：优先重做前3秒、产品露出和卖点表达；</li>
<li><strong>点击正常、转化低</strong>：检查价格、运费、优惠、评价、商品页信息和付款体验；</li>
<li><strong>转化正常、订单成本高</strong>：检查受众定向、出价、预算节奏和竞争强度；</li>
<li><strong>账户频繁审核或限制</strong>：检查商品资质、达人授权、素材版权、促销声明和落地页一致性。</li>
</ul>
<p>不要因为单条素材消耗较低就立即关停。短视频广告需要足够的展示和转化样本；但若素材明显缺乏点击，也没有必要持续追加预算。</p>
<h2>6. 冷启动结束后，哪些素材应该进入长期素材池？</h2>
<p>保留数据表现稳定、授权清晰的素材，并建立可持续迭代机制。每个有效素材至少尝试拆出三个新版本：更换开头、补充一条用户问题，或替换使用场景。</p>
<p>同时保留“失败素材库”，记录它对应的卖点、开头、受众和消耗原因。失败素材并不是完全无价值：当商品价格、促销或落地页发生变化时，原有素材可以重新测试。</p>
<p>新品冷启动的核心不是押中一条爆款，而是尽快建立可复用的测试系统：素材有编号，变更有记录，数据能归因，账户与余额有人管理。这样后续扩量时，团队放大的是已经被验证的创意，而不是未经验证的运气。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>For a TikTok Shop product launch, more creative does not automatically mean better performance. A useful creative pool gives every video a specific job: stop the scroll, explain the benefit, demonstrate value, generate clicks, or drive purchases.</p>
<h2>1. How many creatives should a new product have at launch?</h2>
<p>Start with <strong>15–25 core creatives</strong>, rather than producing hundreds in advance. During cold start, the priority is to identify effective combinations quickly, not to increase volume for its own sake.</p>
<p>A practical structure is:</p>
<ul>
<li>3–5 product-opening videos focused on the product, use case, or customer pain point;</li>
<li>3–5 demonstrations explaining what the product does;</li>
<li>3–5 customer-proof videos using genuine feedback, before-and-after comparisons, or reviews;</li>
<li>3–5 promotional videos explaining bundles, discounts, or limited-time benefits;</li>
<li>2–5 creator-style videos suitable for authorized ads or Spark Ads testing, where available.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is a starting range, not a universal rule. High-consideration products may need more tutorials, comparisons, and FAQ content. Impulse-driven products can be tested more heavily across hooks, settings, and creator styles.</p>
<h2>2. Should each creative focus on one benefit or present the whole product?\n</h2>
<p>Use <strong>one core benefit per creative</strong> during the initial test. TikTok users are more likely to engage with a specific problem or scenario before they process a full product description.</p>
<p>For example, a portable blender could be tested through five angles: quick breakfast preparation for time-pressed commuters, easy cleaning, suitable capacity for travel, actual blending performance, or differences from a standard bottle. A 15-second video should not attempt to cover materials, capacity, discounts, and brand history at the same time.</p>
<p>Focused creatives also produce clearer insight. A team can identify which problem attracts attention instead of merely knowing that a video generated some purchases.</p>
<h2>3. Should creatives be tested organically before advertising?</h2>
<p>Run organic publishing and paid testing in parallel. Organic results can reveal useful signals about opening retention and audience feedback, but they should not replace advertising data because organic distribution is affected by account history, recommendation conditions, and audience composition.</p>
<p>Before launch, assign an ID to every creative and test comparable groups with similar copy, targeting, and bidding. Compare click-through rate, watch time, conversion rate, and cost per order—not simply whether delivery stopped because of the bid.</p>
<p>Change only one major variable per test. If the hook is being evaluated, keep the video length, music, landing page, and offer relatively consistent. Otherwise, even a winning result will not explain why it worked.</p>
<h2>4. How should creatives be distributed across TikTok Shop accounts?</h2>
<p>It is generally a mistake to create numerous accounts solely to access more opportunities. Fragmented accounts complicate recharge, balance monitoring, permissions, attribution, and budget control.</p>
<p>A better approach is:</p>
<ul>
<li>Use one main account for product exploration and core sales;</li>
<li>Add a limited number of accounts only where separate markets, creator-authorized campaigns, or independent budgets justify the separation;</li>
<li>Manage creator content through official authorization tools where possible rather than repeatedly opening accounts or repurposing content without permission;</li>
<li>Maintain a creative registry with asset ID, version, selling point, authorization scope, spend, and order sources;</li>
<li>Assign balance alerts, recharge responsibilities, and daily spending limits to each ad account.</li>
</ul>
<p>When a strong creative is identified, produce a second batch based on the same insight. Changing accounts alone is not a creative strategy. Any cross-account rollout should also preserve consistent authorization, landing pages, and promotional claims.</p>
<h2>5. Is underperformance a creative problem or an account problem?\n</h2>
<p>Diagnose the stage where the performance breaks down:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Low watch time and click-through rate:</strong> revise the first three seconds, product visibility, and benefit framing;</li>
<li><strong>Strong clicks but weak conversion:</strong> review price, shipping, offers, reviews, product-page content, and checkout experience;</li>
<li><strong>Good conversion but high order cost:</strong> examine targeting, bidding, budget pacing, and competitive pressure;</li>
<li><strong>Reviews, restrictions, or account interruptions:</strong> check product documentation, creator authorization, media rights, promotional claims, and landing-page consistency.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not stop every asset merely because its initial spend is low, but do not keep funding a video with no evidence of click intent. Give the test enough impressions and conversion signals to make a reasonable judgment.</p>
<h2>6. Which creatives should move into the long-term library?</h2>
<p>Retain consistently performing assets with clear authorization and continue building variations. For each proven concept, create at least three new versions by changing the opening, adding a customer question, or replacing the use case.</p>
<p>Also maintain a rejected-creative archive. Record the selling point, hook, audience, spend, and likely reason for failure. These assets can be reconsidered when pricing, promotions, or product pages change.</p>
<p>The goal of a cold start is not to guess one viral video. It is to build a reusable testing system: every asset has an ID, every change is documented, performance can be attributed, and accounts and balances are actively managed. Scale proven ideas rather than unverified assumptions.</p>
